@c ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

This chapter explains what CoreUtils is, how it relates (and does not relate)
to GNU Coreutils, and the principles that guide the project.

@menu
* What CoreUtils Is::
* Independence from GNU Coreutils::
* Design Philosophy::
* Scope and Completeness::
* Documentation Conventions::
@end menu

@node What CoreUtils Is
@section What CoreUtils Is

CoreUtils is a self-contained collection of small command-line programs that
perform common file, text, and system tasks.  Every program is a separate
executable built from a single Zig source file.  The tools share a common
build configuration and a common naming and behavior model, but they are
otherwise independent programs.

The collection currently provides 40 utilities:

@itemize
@item
file and directory operations such as @command{cp}, @command{mv},
@command{rm}, @command{mkdir}, @command{chmod}, and @command{stat};
@item
text processing tools such as @command{head}, @command{tail},
@command{cut}, @command{uniq}, @command{cmp}, and @command{base64};
@item
shell and environment helpers such as @command{echo}, @command{env},
@command{test}, @command{sleep}, and @command{yes}.
@end itemize

The project is written in Zig 0.17.0 and links against the C standard library.
It has no third-party dependencies: it uses only the Zig standard library and
the platform C library.

@node Independence from GNU Coreutils
@section Independence from GNU Coreutils

CoreUtils is an independent implementation.  It is @emph{not} GNU Coreutils,
it is @emph{not} a fork of GNU Coreutils, and it is @emph{not} part of the GNU
Project.  The project is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by the
Free Software Foundation or the GNU Project.

Some utility names in this manual (for example, @command{cp}, @command{echo},
@command{test}, or @command{stat}) are names of long-established Unix
commands.  Using the same name makes the tools convenient in a shell, but it
does not imply that the behavior is identical to any other implementation.
Where CoreUtils differs from the traditional behavior of a utility, the
difference is documented in that utility's section and summarized in
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node Design Philosophy
@section Design Philosophy

The project is guided by the following principles.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Small and self-contained.}  Each utility is one file with a single
@code{main} function.  There is no shared runtime library beyond the Zig
standard library and libc.
@item
@strong{Predictable build.}  A single @command{zig build} command produces
every utility.
@item
@strong{Honest scope.}  Utilities implement a focused subset of their
traditional behavior.  Unsupported options are rejected rather than silently
ignored (with a small number of documented exceptions).
@item
@strong{No false compatibility claims.}  This manual describes what the code
does, not what a differently named program is expected to do.
@end itemize

@node Scope and Completeness
@section Scope and Completeness

CoreUtils does not aim to reproduce all of GNU Coreutils or to be a drop-in
replacement for the POSIX utilities.  It implements a useful subset and
documents the boundaries clearly.  In particular:

@itemize
@item
Some utilities accept only a subset of the options found in other
implementations.
@item
Some utilities have different output formats (for example, @command{wc} and
@command{stat}).
@item
This release has known defects that affect output and, in one case, cause a
crash.  They are listed in @ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.
@end itemize

@node Documentation Conventions
@section Documentation Conventions

@itemize @bullet
@item
Option names are shown as @option{-x} or @option{--long-option}.
@item
Placeholders that you replace are written in uppercase, such as
@var{FILE} or @var{N}.
@item
Sample shell sessions are shown in monospaced text.  A leading @samp{$} marks
the shell prompt; it is not part of the command.
@item
Where the current release does @emph{not} behave as the synopsis suggests,
the actual behavior is stated explicitly in a @strong{Known limitations}
paragraph and referenced to the list in
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.
@end itemize

@c ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@node System Requirements
@chapter System Requirements

@cindex requirements
@cindex Zig version

CoreUtils is built and run with the following requirements.

@table @asis
@item Zig
Version 0.17.0.  The build uses the standard Zig build system
(@file{build.zig}) and does not rely on a @file{build.zig.zon} package
manifest; there are no external Zig dependencies.

@item Operating system
A Unix-like system.  The project has been built and exercised on Linux
(x86_64).  Several utilities call POSIX or Linux C library functions and rely
on the Linux x86_64 layout of @code{struct stat}, so portability to other
platforms is not claimed and has not been verified.

@item C library
A C standard library (libc).  Each executable is built with C library linking
enabled (@code{link_libc = true} in @file{build.zig}) because several
utilities use libc functions such as @code{getpwuid}, @code{getlogin},
@code{utimensat}, @code{chmod}, @code{symlink}, @code{link}, @code{access},
@code{setenv}, @code{execvp}, and @code{strftime}.

@item Tools for the documentation
Building this manual requires GNU Texinfo (the @command{makeinfo} or
@command{texi2any} program).  Producing a PDF additionally requires a TeX
installation.  See @file{docs/README.md} for details.
@end table

