@node Building and Installing
@chapter Building and Installing

@menu
* Building::
* Installing::
* Running the Utilities::
@end menu

@node Building
@section Building

From the project root directory, run:

@example
zig build
@end example

This compiles every utility listed in the @code{utils} array in
@file{build.zig} and installs the executables into @file{zig-out/bin/}.  No
network access and no third-party packages are required.

The optimize mode can be selected with the standard Zig option:

@example
zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast
@end example

Valid values are @code{Debug}, @code{ReleaseSafe}, @code{ReleaseFast}, and
@code{ReleaseSmall}.  The build configuration sets @code{ReleaseSmall} as its
preferred optimize mode, which is used when @option{--release} is passed
without an explicit @option{-Doptimize}.

A cross target or a custom target can be selected with the standard
@option{-Dtarget} option, although see @ref{System Requirements} regarding
supported platforms.

@node Installing
@section Installing

By default, @command{zig build} installs into the @file{zig-out} directory
inside the project.  To install into a different prefix, use the standard Zig
@option{--prefix} option:

@example
zig build --prefix /usr/local
@end example

This installs the executables into @file{/usr/local/bin}.  You may need
elevated privileges to write to system directories.

No shell completions, manual pages, or package-manager integration are
generated by the build.  This Texinfo manual and the license file are part of
the source tree; install them manually if you package the project.

@node Running the Utilities
@section Running the Utilities

After a default build, run a utility from @file{zig-out/bin/}.  For example:

@example
./zig-out/bin/echo "Hello, world"
@end example

If you installed into a directory that is on your @env{PATH}, you can invoke
the utilities by name:

@example
echo "Hello, world" | ./zig-out/bin/wc /dev/stdin
@end example

Note that several utilities in this release write their output to standard
error rather than standard output, and some do not flush standard output at
all.  Read @ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences} before relying on
pipeline output.

