@end menu

@node basename
@section basename
@cindex basename

@command{basename} prints a path name with any leading directory components
removed, and optionally removes a trailing suffix.

@example
basename @var{NAME} [@var{SUFFIX}]
basename @var{OPTION}... @var{NAME}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -a
Treat every operand as a @var{NAME} rather than as a single name plus suffix.
@item -s @var{SUFFIX}
Remove a trailing @var{SUFFIX} from each result.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

Trailing slashes are removed before the last component is selected.  A path
consisting only of slashes is reduced to @samp{/}; an empty operand is
replaced by @samp{.}.  A suffix is removed only when the remaining base name
would be non-empty.

@example
$ basename /usr/bin/zig
@end example

@noindent
The intended output is @samp{zig}.  @strong{Current behavior:} the output
does not appear, because the standard output writer is never flushed; see
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@strong{Known limitations:} the @option{-z}/@option{--zero} option and other
long options are not implemented; output is written to a buffered standard
output stream that is not flushed.

@node catty
@section catty
@cindex catty

@command{catty} is a minimal file reader.  It opens a single file, reads at
most 4096 bytes, and prints those bytes followed by a newline.

@example
catty @var{FILE}
catty --version
@end example

@table @option
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

@command{catty} does not accept multiple files, option flags such as
@option{-n}, or standard input.  Its output is written to standard error
because it uses the Zig debugging print routine.

@strong{Known limitations:} only the first 4096 bytes of the file are
printed; a newline is always appended; there is no @option{--help}; and an
unreadable or missing file produces an unhandled error trace.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node chmod
@section chmod
@cindex chmod

@command{chmod} changes the mode (permission) bits of files.

@example
chmod [@var{OPTION}]... @var{MODE} @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -c, --changes
Report only files whose mode actually changes.
@item -f, --silent
Suppress most error messages.  @option{--quiet} is accepted as a synonym.
@item -v, --verbose
Report every file that is processed.
@item -R, --recursive
Accepted for compatibility but @strong{not implemented}.  Using it produces
an error and exit status 1.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

@var{MODE} may be either an octal number (for example, @samp{755} or
@samp{0644}) or a symbolic expression.  Symbolic expressions use the letters
@samp{u}, @samp{g}, @samp{o}, and @samp{a} for the user, group, other, and all
classes; the operators @samp{+}, @samp{-}, and @samp{=}; and the permission
letters @samp{r}, @samp{w}, @samp{x}, @samp{X}, @samp{s}, and @samp{t}.
Permission bits can also be copied between classes with @samp{u}, @samp{g},
and @samp{o} as the operation argument.  Commas separate multiple clauses.

@example
$ chmod 644 notes.txt
$ chmod u+x build.sh
$ chmod -v go-w report.txt
mode of 'report.txt' changed to 0644 (-rw-r--r--)
@end example

@noindent
Verbose output is written to standard output.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if the mode is invalid, if an operand
is missing, or if any file could not be changed.

@strong{Known limitations:} @option{-R} is not implemented; the file-type
character in the verbose mode string is always @samp{-}; there is no
@option{--reference} option; symbolic modes operate on the mode reported by
@code{lstat}, so symlinks themselves are not followed.

@node cp
@section cp
@cindex cp

@command{cp} copies a file or a directory tree.

@example
cp @var{SOURCE} @var{DEST}
cp --version
@end example

@table @option
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

If @var{SOURCE} is a directory, its contents are copied recursively,
including subdirectories, regular files, and symbolic links.  Other entry
types are skipped and a message is printed.  If @var{DEST} is an existing
directory, the base name of @var{SOURCE} is appended to it.  Missing parent
directories of the destination are created.  On success the program prints:

@example
Copied 'source' -> 'dest'
@end example

@noindent
This message is written to standard error.

@strong{Known limitations:} exactly two operands are accepted; there is no
@option{-r}/@option{-R} option (recursion is automatic), no
@option{-f}/@option{-i}/@option{-n} conflict policy, no @option{-p} attribute
preservation, and no support for copying multiple sources into a directory.
See @ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences} regarding the output stream.

@node dirname
@section dirname
@cindex dirname

@command{dirname} prints the directory portion of each path name.

@example
dirname @var{NAME}...
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

Trailing slashes are removed; a path consisting only of slashes yields
@samp{/}; a path with no slash yields @samp{.}.

@example
$ dirname /usr/bin/zig
@end example

@noindent
The intended output is @samp{/usr/bin}.  @strong{Current behavior:} the
output does not appear because the standard output writer is not flushed; see
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@strong{Known limitations:} no @option{-z}/@option{--zero} option; output is
not flushed.

@node ll
@section ll
@cindex ll

@command{ll} is a compact directory lister.  It is inspired by a long
directory listing but is not compatible with @command{ls}.

@example
ll [@var{OPTION}]... [@var{PATH}]
@end example

@table @option
@item -a
Include entries whose names begin with a dot.
@item -h
Show sizes in human-readable units (B, K, M, G, T, P).
@item -l
Accepted and ignored.
@end table

The directory is opened (the current directory by default) and entries are
listed sorted by name.  A header line reports the number of entries:

@example
$ ll /usr/include
total 12 entries
d       4  Jan  3 09:14  .
...
@end example

@noindent
Each line shows a one-character file type, the size right-aligned in eight
columns, a timestamp in @samp{Mon DD HH:MM} form, and the entry name.
Symbolic links are followed by @samp{-> @var{target}}.  Output is written to
standard error.

@strong{Known limitations:} permissions, owner, group, and link counts are
not shown; the @samp{total} value is a count of entries rather than a count of
disk blocks; years and seconds are omitted from the timestamp; only the first
non-option path is used; and @option{-l} has no effect.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences} regarding the output stream.

@node ln
@section ln
@cindex ln

@command{ln} creates hard links or symbolic links.

@example
ln [@var{OPTION}]... @var{TARGET} [@var{LINK_NAME}]
@end example

@table @option
@item -s, --symbolic
Create a symbolic link instead of a hard link.
@item -f, --force
Remove an existing destination before creating the link.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

If @var{LINK_NAME} is omitted, the base name of @var{TARGET} is used.  The
target string is stored exactly as given.

@example
$ ln -s /usr/bin/zig latest-zig
$ ln report.txt report-backup.txt
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if the link could not be created or if
an option is invalid.

@strong{Known limitations:} linking several targets into a directory is not
implemented; giving more than two operands produces an error.  Long options
such as @option{-v}, @option{-b}, and @option{-T} are not supported.

@node mkdir
@section mkdir
@cindex mkdir

@command{mkdir} creates directories.

@example
mkdir [@var{OPTION}]... @var{DIRECTORY}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -m @var{MODE}
Set the mode of the created directories.  @var{MODE} must be an octal number
(for example, @samp{700} or @samp{0755}); symbolic modes are not accepted.
@item -p
Do not report an error if the directory already exists, and create missing
parent directories as needed.
@item -v
Print a message for each directory created.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

@example
$ mkdir -p build/out
$ mkdir -m 700 secrets
$ mkdir -v logs
mkdir: created directory 'logs'
@end example

@noindent
Verbose messages are written to standard output.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if any directory could not be created
or if an option is invalid.

@strong{Known limitations:} @option{-m} accepts only octal modes; the long
forms @option{--mode} and @option{--verbose} are not accepted; there is no
@option{-Z} SELinux context option.

@node mv
@section mv
@cindex mv

@command{mv} moves or renames a file or directory.

@example
mv @var{SOURCE} @var{DEST}
mv --version
@end example

@table @option
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

The program first attempts an atomic rename.  If the source and destination
are on different filesystems, it falls back to copying the source and then
deleting it.  If @var{DEST} is an existing directory, the base name of
@var{SOURCE} is appended to it.  Missing parent directories of the
destination are created.  On success it prints:

@example
Moved 'source' -> 'dest'
@end example

@noindent
This message is written to standard error.

@strong{Known limitations:} exactly two operands are accepted; there is no
@option{-f}/@option{-i}/@option{-n} conflict policy, no @option{-t}/@option{-T},
and no @option{-v}.  The cross-device fallback skips special file types.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences} regarding the output stream.

@node pwd
@section pwd
@cindex pwd

@command{pwd} prints the full path name of the current working directory.

@example
pwd
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

The path is obtained from the operating system (a physical path).  Output is
written to standard output.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if the current directory cannot be
determined.

@strong{Known limitations:} there is no @option{-L} versus @option{-P}
distinction, and no @option{--version} option.

@node rm
@section rm
@cindex rm

@command{rm} removes files.

@example
rm @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --version
Print version information.
@end table

Each operand is removed with the operating system's file-deletion call.  On
success the program prints:

@example
removed 'file'
@end example

@noindent
Errors are printed for files that cannot be removed, and processing
continues with the remaining operands.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 if all files were removed; nonzero if any removal
failed.

@strong{Known limitations:} @command{rm} does not remove directories (an
@code{IsDir} error is reported); there is no @option{-r}/@option{-R} recursive
mode, no @option{-f}, @option{-i}, or @option{-d}, and no
@option{--preserve-root}.  Removal notices and errors are written to standard
error, and a failed removal also prints a Zig error trace.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node rmdir
@section rmdir
@cindex rmdir

@command{rmdir} removes empty directories.

@example
rmdir [@var{OPTION}]... @var{DIRECTORY}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -p, --parents
Also remove each parent directory that becomes empty.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --version
Print version information.
@end table

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if any directory could not be removed.

@strong{Known limitations:} only empty directories can be removed.  The
@option{-p} option is currently broken: it triggers a runtime panic
(@code{@@memcpy arguments alias}).  The message printed when no directory is
given contains a stray word, @samp{butuh}.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node stat
@section stat
@cindex stat

@command{stat} displays status information about a file.

@example
stat [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -L, --dereference
Follow symbolic links.
@item -c @var{FORMAT}, --format=@var{FORMAT}
Print information according to @var{FORMAT} instead of the default listing.
@end table

Without a format, a multi-line listing is produced.  Timestamps are printed as
raw Unix epoch seconds rather than as formatted dates:

@example
$ stat notes.txt
  File: notes.txt
  Size: 4	Blocks: 8	IO Block: 4096	regular file
Device: 1dh/29d	Inode: 546	Links: 1
Access: (664/-rw-rw-r--)	Uid: (1000)	Gid: (1000)
Access: 1791645335
Modify: 1791645335
Change: 1791645335
@end example

The following format directives are supported:

@table @code
@item %n
File name.
@item %s
Total size in bytes.
@item %F
File type (for example, @samp{regular file} or @samp{directory}).
@item %a
Access rights in octal.
@item %A
Access rights in human-readable form.
@item %u
Numeric user ID.
@item %g
Numeric group ID.
@item %U
User name of the owner.
@item %G
Group name of the owner.
@item %%
A literal percent sign.
@end table

@example
$ stat -c '%n %s %a %F' notes.txt
notes.txt 4 664 regular file
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if any file could not be examined.

@strong{Known limitations:} there is no filesystem mode (@option{-f}), no
terse mode (@option{-t}), and no @option{--printf}.  The @code{struct stat}
layout used is specific to Linux x86_64, so the reported device and inode
values are only meaningful on that platform.

@node sync
@section sync
@cindex sync

@command{sync} flushes pending filesystem buffers to disk.

@example
sync
@end example

The program calls the operating system's synchronization call and exits with
status 0.  It accepts no options and prints nothing.

@strong{Known limitations:} @option{--help}, @option{--version}, and data
selection options are not supported.

@node tee
@section tee
@cindex tee

@command{tee} copies standard input to standard output and to one or more
files.

@example
tee [@var{OPTION}]... [@var{FILE}]...
@end example

@table @option
@item -a, --append
Append to the named files instead of overwriting them.
@item -i, --ignore-interrupts
Accepted for compatibility but has no effect.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

Data is read in blocks and written to standard output and to every named
file.

@example
$ printf 'log line\n' | tee build.log
log line
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if a file could not be opened or
written.

@strong{Known limitations:} @option{-i} does not actually change signal
handling; the tool does not provide the @option{--output-error} family of
options.

@node touch
@section touch
@cindex touch

@command{touch} changes file access and modification times and creates empty
files if necessary.

@example
touch [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -a
Change only the access time.
@item -m
Change only the modification time.
@item -c, --no-create
Do not create files that do not exist.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

The program creates a missing file (unless @option{-c} is given) and then sets
the requested times to the current time.

@example
$ touch new-empty-file
$ touch -a access-only
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if any file could not be touched.

@strong{Known limitations:} timestamps cannot be set to an explicit value;
there is no @option{-d}, @option{-t}, @option{-r}, @option{--time}, or
@option{--date}.  Specifying both @option{-a} and @option{-m} results in
neither time being changed, because both are marked as omitted.

@node truncate
@section truncate
@cindex truncate

@command{truncate} shrinks or extends the size of a file.

@example
truncate [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -s @var{SIZE}, --size @var{SIZE}
Set or adjust the file size.  A suffix of @samp{K}, @samp{M}, or @samp{G}
multiplies the value by 1024, 1024@sup{2}, or 1024@sup{3} respectively.  The
value must be non-negative.
@item -c, --no-create
Do not create files that do not exist.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

@example
$ truncate -s 1M disk-image.bin
$ truncate -s0 empty.txt
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if the size is missing or invalid, if no
file is given, or if any file could not be modified.

@strong{Known limitations:} the form @option{--size=@var{SIZE}} (with an
equals sign) is @strong{not} accepted; use @option{-s @var{SIZE}},
@option{--size @var{SIZE}}, or @option{-s@var{SIZE}}.  When the target file
does not exist, the file is created but left at size 0; the requested size is
not applied.  Relative sizes (with a leading @samp{+}, @samp{-}, or @samp{%})
and @option{--reference} are not supported.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@c ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Text Processing Utilities
@chapter Text Processing Utilities

These utilities read text and produce transformed text output.

@menu
* base64::
* cksum::
* cmp::
* cut::
* head::
* lsort::
* printf::
* seq::
* tail::
* uniq::
* wc::
@end menu

@node base64
@section base64
@cindex base64

@command{base64} encodes or decodes data in the standard Base64 alphabet.

@example
base64 [@var{OPTION}]... [@var{FILE}]
@end example

@table @option
@item -d, --decode
Decode data instead of encoding it.
@item -w @var{COLS}, --wrap @var{COLS}
Wrap encoded output after @var{COLS} characters.  The default is 76; use 0 to
disable wrapping.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

If no file is given, standard input is read.  Multiple input files are
concatenated.  Decoding ignores spaces, tabs, and line breaks.

@example
$ printf 'hello' | base64
aGVsbG8=
$ printf 'aGVsbG8=' | base64 -d
hello
@end example

@strong{Known limitations:} the form @option{--wrap=@var{COLS}} (with an
equals sign) is not accepted; use @option{-w @var{COLS}} or
@option{-w@var{COLS}}.  Reading to-be-decoded data from standard input with
@option{base64 -d} and no file operand fails, because the option is mistaken
for a file name; pass an explicit file or use @code{base64 -d /dev/stdin}.
Invalid encoded input produces an uncaught error.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node cksum
@section cksum
@cindex cksum

@command{cksum} computes a message digest or checksum of a file, or prints its
Base64 encoding.

@example
cksum --mode @var{ALGORITHM} @var{FILE}
@end example

@table @option
@item --mode @var{ALGORITHM}
Select the algorithm.  Required.
@item -h, --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

The following algorithms are available:

@table @code
@item md5
MD5 (128-bit).
@item sha1
SHA-1 (160-bit).
@item sha256
SHA-256 (256-bit).
@item sha512
SHA-512 (512-bit).
@item sha3
SHA3-256 (256-bit).
@item blake2
BLAKE2b-512 (512-bit).
@item crc32
CRC-32 (32-bit).
@item base64
Base64 encoding (not a checksum).
@end table

Hash and CRC results are printed as a hexadecimal digest, two spaces, and the
file name.  The @code{base64} mode prints only the encoded data.

@example
$ cksum --mode sha256 notes.txt
09834d488008f5f1ef589a2d7cedc52425bee9dd23b2212e4c1d673c5cbb54e4  notes.txt
$ cksum --mode crc32 notes.txt
9c70f918  notes.txt
@end example

@noindent
All output is written to standard error.

@strong{Known limitations:} this is not the interface of the traditional
@command{cksum} utility; @option{--mode} is mandatory, and @option{--version}
is recognized only as the first argument.  Input is limited to 64 MiB.  There
is no standard-input mode.  See @ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}
regarding the output stream.

@node cmp
@section cmp
@cindex cmp

@command{cmp} compares two files byte by byte.

@example
cmp [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FILE1} [@var{FILE2}]
@end example

@table @option
@item -s, --silent, --quiet
Suppress all normal output and report only the exit status.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

If @var{FILE2} is omitted or is @samp{-}, standard input is used.  The first
difference is reported with its byte offset and line number; if the files
differ in length, the shorter file is reported.

@example
$ cmp a.txt b.txt
a.txt b.txt differ: byte 1, line 1
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 if the files are identical; 1 if they differ; 2 on
trouble (a missing file, an invalid option, or a missing operand).

@strong{Known limitations:} only the first difference is reported; there is no
@option{-l}, @option{-b}, @option{-i}, or @option{-n} option.

@node cut
@section cut
@cindex cut

@command{cut} prints selected bytes, characters, or fields from each line of
its input.

@example
cut @var{OPTION}... [@var{FILE}]...
@end example

@table @option
@item -b @var{LIST}
Select the bytes in @var{LIST}.
@item -c @var{LIST}
Select the characters in @var{LIST}.
@item -f @var{LIST}
Select the fields in @var{LIST}.
@item -d @var{DELIM}
Use the single-byte @var{DELIM} instead of TAB as the field delimiter.
@item -s
Do not print lines that do not contain the delimiter.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

@var{LIST} is a comma-separated list of ranges.  Each range is one of
@var{N} (a single position), @var{N}- (from @var{N} to the end), -@var{M}
(from the beginning to @var{M}), or @var{N}-@var{M}.  If no file operand is
given, standard input is read.

@example
$ printf 'one:two:three\n' | cut -d: -f2
two
$ printf 'abcdef\n' | cut -c2-4
bcd
@end example

@strong{Known limitations:} @option{-c} is byte-oriented and does not handle
multibyte characters; there is no @option{--complement},
@option{--output-delimiter}, or @option{-z}; and option ordering matters when
@option{-s} follows a list option, in which case @option{-s} may be mistaken
for a file name.  See @ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node head
@section head
@cindex head

@command{head} prints the first lines of each file.

@example
head [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -n @var{N}, --lines @var{N}
Print the first @var{N} lines.  The default is 10.  The form -@var{N} is also
accepted.
@item -h, --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

When more than one file is given, each file's output is preceded by a header
of the form @samp{==> @var{FILE} <==}, with a blank line between files.

@example
$ head -n 2 notes.txt
hello
world
@end example

@noindent
The lines are written to standard error in this release.

@strong{Known limitations:} each file is read entirely into memory (with a
64 MiB limit); output is written to standard error; the program exits with
status 0 even if a file is missing or cannot be read; and there is no
@option{-c}, @option{-q}, or @option{-v}.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node lsort
@section lsort
@cindex lsort

@command{lsort} sorts the lines of a file in byte order and prints them.

@example
lsort @var{FILE}
lsort --version
@end example

@table @option
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

The comparison is a plain byte comparison of whole lines, not a locale-aware
or field-aware comparison.

@example
$ lsort unsorted.txt
a
b
c
@end example

@noindent
Output is written to standard output.

@strong{Known limitations:} only one file is accepted and there is no
standard-input mode; there are no numeric, reverse, unique, or key options;
the tool is not the traditional @command{sort} utility and does not aim for
compatibility with it.

@node printf
@section printf
@cindex printf

@command{printf} formats and prints its arguments.

@example
printf @var{FORMAT} [@var{ARGUMENT}]...
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

The following conversions are supported:

@table @code
@item %s
A string.
@item %d
A signed decimal integer.
@item %i
A signed decimal integer (synonym for @code{%d}).
@item %u
An unsigned decimal integer.
@item %x
A lowercase hexadecimal integer.
@item %X
An uppercase hexadecimal integer.
@item %o
An octal integer.
@item %c
The first character of a string.
@item %b
A string in which backslash escapes are interpreted.
@item %%
A literal percent sign.
@end table

The escape sequences @code{\n}, @code{\t}, @code{\r}, @code{\\}, and
@code{\0@var{NNN}} (up to three octal digits) are recognized in @var{FORMAT}.
The format is reused until all arguments have been consumed; a missing
argument is treated as an empty string or as 0.

@example
$ printf '%s=%d\n' answer 42
answer=42
@end example

@strong{Known limitations:} there is no field-width, precision, or flag
support; there is no @code{%f}, @code{%e}, or @code{%g}; there is no @samp{*}
width argument; and an unknown conversion is printed literally.

@node seq
@section seq
@cindex seq

@command{seq} prints a sequence of numbers.

@example
seq [@var{OPTION}]... @var{LAST}
seq [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FIRST} @var{LAST}
seq [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FIRST} @var{INCREMENT} @var{LAST}
@end example

@table @option
@item -s @var{STRING}, --separator @var{STRING}
Use @var{STRING} to separate numbers.  The default is a newline.
@item -w, --equal-width
Accepted for compatibility but @strong{not implemented}.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

The operands may be floating-point numbers.  A zero increment is rejected.
After the last number, a newline is always written.

@example
$ seq 1 5
@end example

@noindent
The intended output is the numbers 1 through 5, each on its own line.
@strong{Current behavior:} the output does not appear when it fits within the
internal 4 KiB buffer, because standard output is never flushed.  Larger
sequences are emitted only in part.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@strong{Known limitations:} @option{-w} is ignored; @option{--separator=}
(with an equals sign) is not accepted; there is no format string; the output
is not flushed.

@node tail
@section tail
@cindex tail

@command{tail} prints the last lines of each file.

@example
tail [@var{OPTION}]... @var{FILE}...
@end example

@table @option
@item -n @var{N}, --lines @var{N}
Print the last @var{N} lines.  The default is 10.  The form -@var{N} is also
accepted.
@item -h, --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

When more than one file is given, each file's output is preceded by a header
of the form @samp{==> @var{FILE} <==}, with a blank line between files.

@example
$ tail -n 2 notes.txt
hello
world
@end example

@noindent
The lines are written to standard error in this release.

@strong{Known limitations:} there is no follow mode (@option{-f}); each file
is read entirely into memory (with a 64 MiB limit); output is written to
standard error; the program exits with status 0 even if a file is missing; and
there is no @option{-c}, @option{-q}, or @option{-v}.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node uniq
@section uniq
@cindex uniq

@command{uniq} filters adjacent duplicate lines.

@example
uniq [@var{OPTION}]... [@var{INPUT} [@var{OUTPUT}]]
@end example

@table @option
@item -c, --count
Prefix each output line with its number of occurrences.
@item -d, --repeated
Print only lines that occur more than once.
@item -u, --unique
Print only lines that occur exactly once.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing.
@end table

Only adjacent duplicate lines are considered equal, so the input is normally
sorted first.  With counts, the format is a right-aligned count in seven
columns, a space, and the line.

@strong{Known limitations:} the @var{OUTPUT} operand is accepted but ignored
(output always goes to standard output).  Standard output is never flushed, so
in practice most output does not appear.  Invoking the tool with options but
no file operand (for example, @command{uniq -c}) fails because the option is
mistaken for a file name.  Only adjacent duplicates are detected.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node wc
@section wc
@cindex wc

@command{wc} counts the lines, words, and characters in a file.

@example
wc @var{FILE}
@end example

@table @option
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

The output has the form:

@example
$ wc notes.txt
Lines: 2, Words: 3, Characters: 14
@end example

@noindent
This output is written to standard error.

Words are separated by spaces, tabs, or carriage returns.  The character
count is computed by adding one newline byte to each line, so a final line
that lacks a trailing newline is counted one byte too high.  Lines are
counted as delimiter-separated segments, so a final unterminated line is
counted as a line.

@strong{Known limitations:} no option flags such as @option{-l},
@option{-w}, @option{-c}, @option{-m}, or @option{-L}; only a single file is
accepted and there is no standard-input mode; the counts differ from the
traditional @command{wc}; and the @option{--version} banner is mislabeled
(it prints the name @samp{Echo}).  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@c ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Shell and Environment Utilities
@chapter Shell and Environment Utilities

These utilities deal with command output, the process environment, and short
system queries.

@menu
* date::
* echo::
* env::
* false::
* logname::
* nproc::
* printenv::
* sleep::
* test::
* true::
* whoami::
* yes::
@end menu

@node date
@section date
@cindex date

@command{date} displays the current date and time.

@example
date [@var{OPTION}]... [+@var{FORMAT}]
@end example

@table @option
@item -u, --utc, --universal
Print Coordinated Universal Time instead of local time.
@item -R, --rfc-email
Print the date in RFC 5322 (@samp{RFC email}) format.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

@var{FORMAT} is a @code{strftime} format string and must begin with a plus
sign.  Without a format, the default is
@samp{%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y} for local time, or the UTC equivalent when
@option{-u} is given.

@example
$ date
Sat Oct 10 22:17:55 +07 2026
$ date -u
Sat Oct 10 15:17:55 UTC 2026
$ date -R
Sat, 10 Oct 2026 22:17:55 +0700
@end example

@noindent
Output is written to standard output.

@strong{Known limitations:} @command{date} can only display the time; it
cannot set the clock.  There is no @option{-d}, @option{-f}, @option{-r},
@option{--date}, or @option{--iso-8601}, and no @option{--version}.  A format
string is truncated to 255 bytes.

@node echo
@section echo
@cindex echo

@command{echo} prints its arguments separated by single spaces and followed
by a newline.

@example
echo @var{WORD}...
@end example

@table @option
@item --version
Print version information.  Recognized only as the first argument.
@end table

@example
$ echo Hello, world
Hello, world
@end example

@noindent
Output is written to standard output.

@strong{Known limitations:} there is no @option{-n} (no trailing newline), no
@option{-e}/@option{-E} (backslash escapes are never interpreted), and no
@option{--help}.  A bare @samp{--help} is printed as an ordinary word.  With
no arguments, a short usage message is written to standard error.

@node env
@section env
@cindex env

@command{env} sets environment variables and then runs a command, or prints
the resulting environment.

@example
env [@var{OPTION}]... [@var{NAME}=@var{VALUE}]... [@var{COMMAND} [@var{ARG}]...]
@end example

@table @option
@item -i, --ignore-environment
Start with an empty environment.
@item -u @var{NAME}, --unset @var{NAME}
Remove @var{NAME} from the environment.  The form @option{-u@var{NAME}} is
also accepted.
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@item --
End option processing; everything after it is the command and its arguments.
@end table

@example
$ env -i FOO=bar
FOO=bar
$ env -u HOME printenv HOME
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 if the environment was printed or the command ran; 127
if the command could not be found.

@strong{Known limitations:} the documented @samp{-} operand (clear the
environment) is not implemented and is treated as a command name.  The form
@option{--unset=@var{NAME}} is not accepted; it is interpreted as a
@var{NAME}=@var{VALUE} assignment.  Any argument containing an equals sign is
treated as an assignment.  There is no @option{-S}, @option{-C}, or
@option{-0}.  See @ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node false
@section false
@cindex false

@command{false} does nothing and returns a failure status.

@example
false
@end example

The program always exits with status 1.  It ignores all arguments and
supports no options.

@node logname
@section logname
@cindex logname

@command{logname} prints the user's login name.

@example
logname
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

The name is obtained from the login name of the controlling terminal.  Output
is written to standard output.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if no login name is available.

@strong{Known limitations:} no @option{--version} option.

@node nproc
@section nproc
@cindex nproc

@command{nproc} prints the number of processing units available to the
current process.

@example
nproc
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

If the count cannot be determined, a value of 1 is printed.  Output is written
to standard output.

@strong{Known limitations:} there is no @option{--all} or @option{--ignore},
and no @option{--version} option.

@node printenv
@section printenv
@cindex printenv

@command{printenv} prints environment variables.

@example
printenv [@var{VARIABLE}]...
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

With no operand, the entire environment is printed as @var{NAME}=@var{VALUE}
lines.  With operands, the value of each named variable is printed.  Output is
written to standard output.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if any named variable is not set.

@strong{Known limitations:} standard output is buffered and never flushed, so
in the current release the program produces no output.  In the
no-argument listing, the order of variables is unspecified.  There is no
@option{-0}/@option{--null} option.  See
@ref{Known Issues and Behavior Differences}.

@node sleep
@section sleep
@cindex sleep

@command{sleep} pauses for a specified duration.

@example
sleep @var{NUMBER}...
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

Each operand is parsed as a floating-point number of seconds; the operands are
summed and the process sleeps for the total.

@example
$ sleep 0.5
$ sleep 1 2
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if an operand is missing or invalid.

@strong{Known limitations:} the built-in help text mentions the suffixes
@samp{s}, @samp{m}, @samp{h}, and @samp{d}, but suffixes are @strong{not}
implemented.  A value such as @samp{1s} is rejected as an invalid time
interval.

@node test
@section test
@cindex test
@cindex [ (test)

@command{test} evaluates a conditional expression and reports the result
through its exit status.  It is also available in bracket form.

@example
test @var{EXPRESSION}
[ @var{EXPRESSION} ]
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.  Recognized only when it is the whole
expression.
@end table

The following primary operators are supported.

File tests:

@table @code
@item -e @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} exists.
@item -f @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a regular file.
@item -d @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a directory.
@item -b @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a block device.
@item -c @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a character device.
@item -p @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a named pipe.
@item -S @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a socket.
@item -s @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} has a size greater than zero.
@item -r @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is readable.
@item -w @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is writable.
@item -x @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is executable.
@item -L @var{FILE}
@var{FILE} is a symbolic link.  @option{-h} is a synonym.
@end table

String and integer tests:

@table @code
@item -z @var{STRING}
The length of @var{STRING} is zero.
@item -n @var{STRING}
The length of @var{STRING} is nonzero.
@item @var{STR1} = @var{STR2}
@itemx @var{STR1} == @var{STR2}
The strings are equal.
@item @var{STR1} != @var{STR2}
The strings are not equal.
@item @var{N1} -eq @var{N2}
@itemx @var{N1} -ne @var{N2}
@itemx @var{N1} -lt @var{N2}
@itemx @var{N1} -le @var{N2}
@itemx @var{N1} -gt @var{N2}
@itemx @var{N1} -ge @var{N2}
Integer comparisons.
@end table

The logical operators @samp{!}, @option{-a} (and), and @option{-o} (or) are
also supported.  @option{-a} binds more tightly than @option{-o}.  With a
single argument, a non-empty string is true and an empty string is false.
The readable, writable, and executable tests use the process's effective
user and group identifiers.

@example
$ test -f /etc/hostname
$ echo $?
0
$ test 5 -gt 3
$ echo $?
0
@end example

@strong{Exit status:} 0 if the expression is true; 1 if it is false; 2 on a
syntax error, a missing operand, or extra arguments.

@strong{Known limitations:} the bracket form requires the program to be
invoked with the name @samp{[}, for example through a symbolic link named
@samp{[} that is found on @env{PATH}; the closing @samp{]} is required.
There is no parenthesis grouping and no @option{-t} terminal test.  The
@option{--help} option is recognized only when it is the sole expression.

@node true
@section true
@cindex true

@command{true} does nothing and returns a success status.

@example
true
@end example

The program always exits with status 0.  It ignores all arguments and
supports no options.

@node whoami
@section whoami
@cindex whoami

@command{whoami} prints the user name associated with the current effective
user ID.

@example
whoami
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

Output is written to standard output.

@strong{Exit status:} 0 on success; 1 if the name cannot be determined.

@strong{Known limitations:} no @option{--version} option.

@node yes
@section yes
@cindex yes

@command{yes} repeatedly outputs a line.

@example
yes [@var{STRING}]...
@end example

@table @option
@item --help
Print a short help message and exit.
@end table

With no operand, the line @samp{y} is repeated.  With operands, the strings
are joined with single spaces and that line is repeated.  The line is limited
to 4096 bytes.  Output continues until writing fails, for example when the
receiving end of a pipe is closed; the program then returns status 0.

@example
$ yes | head -n 3
y
y
y
@end example

@strong{Known limitations:} no @option{--version} option; very long argument
lists are truncated to the 4096-byte line buffer.

