@node Known Issues and Behavior Differences
@chapter Known Issues and Behavior Differences

@cindex known issues
@cindex behavior differences

This chapter lists behavior that is verified in the current release and that
differs from what the utility names and help text might lead you to expect.
The list is based on the source code and on running the built executables.
These issues are documented rather than hidden; fixing them is a welcome
contribution.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Normal output sent to standard error.}  The following utilities write
their ordinary output with the Zig debugging print routine, which targets
standard error, so their output does not appear on standard output:
@command{catty}, @command{cksum}, @command{cp}, @command{head}, @command{ll},
@command{mv}, @command{rm}, @command{rmdir}, @command{tail}, and
@command{wc}.

@item
@strong{Buffered standard output never flushed.}  The following utilities
create a buffered standard output writer and never flush it, so output that
fits within the internal 4 KiB buffer is discarded and larger output may lose
its tail: @command{basename}, @command{dirname}, @command{printenv},
@command{seq}, and @command{uniq}.

@item
@strong{Option tokens mistaken for file names when no file operand is given.}
Several tools leave their internal file-operand index at the first argument
when only options are present, so the option itself is opened as a file.  This
affects @command{base64 -d} reading from standard input, @command{uniq} with
any option reading from standard input, and some orderings of @command{cut}
options.  Passing an explicit file operand works around it.

@item
@strong{@command{truncate} creates zero-length files.}  When the target file
does not exist and file creation is allowed, the file is created but the
requested size is not applied, so the new file is left empty.

@item
@strong{@command{rmdir -p} crashes.}  The @option{-p} implementation copies
overlapping memory and triggers a runtime panic
(@code{@@memcpy arguments alias}).

@item
@strong{@command{rmdir} error message contains a stray word.}  When no
directory is given, the message contains the untranslated word @samp{butuh}.

@item
@strong{@command{seq -w} is ignored.}  The @option{--equal-width} option is
accepted but has no effect, and the output is also subject to the flushing
issue.

@item
@strong{@command{chmod -R} is not implemented.}  The option is accepted by the
parser but reported as unimplemented, with exit status 1.

@item
@strong{@command{ln} cannot link multiple targets.}  More than two operands
produce an error.

@item
@strong{@command{sleep} suffixes are advertised but unsupported.}  The help
text lists @samp{s}, @samp{m}, @samp{h}, and @samp{d} suffixes, but values
such as @samp{1s} are rejected.

@item
@strong{Long options with an equals sign are not always accepted.}
@option{--size=}, @option{--wrap=}, @option{--unset=}, and
@option{--separator=} are not recognized in some utilities even though the
help text shows them.  Use the space-separated or joined short form instead.

@item
@strong{Unhandled errors print Zig error traces.}  A failed removal in
@command{rm}, a hash error in @command{cksum}, or invalid Base64 input in
@command{base64} can produce a Zig error message and stack trace rather than a
quiet diagnostic.

@item
@strong{@command{wc} mislabels its version banner.}  The @option{--version}
output prints the name @samp{Echo}.

@item
@strong{Version banners claim all rights reserved.}  See the note in
@ref{License}; the MIT License governs the project.
@end enumerate

